  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure forming near southern New England this morning will\nmove exit east of Cape Cod during the late afternoon and evening.\nModels continue to show a rapid increase of deep moisture \ntonight into Sunday morning as PWATs climb to 1.5 to 2 inches. \nCAMs and global models have now started to show broad forcing \nfor ascent leading to an axis of rain extending north of the \ndeveloping low across much of NH through this morning which has\nresulted in a slight increase of QPF. This axis of rain will \nthen become focused across southern NH into the coastal plain of\nMaine late morning into the afternoon as areas to the NW see a \nbreak in precipitation. Rain then moves offshore by evening.\n\nA cold front will slide east across Upstate NY this evening \nwith showers and storms forming along the front. These showers \nand storms will approach the CT Valley around sunset while \nthere is potential for lingering MUCAPE to keep chances for \nthunder in the forecast. Instability wanes overnight with mainly\na broken line of showers crossing the area with the cold front.\nTotal QPF will vary by location depending on where convective\nshowers are most persistent. Some areas may only see a quarter\ninch from this, but others up to an inch. Rain chances diminish\nNW to SE as the cold front moves offshore Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure over New England will keep the area dry and \nseasonable. Temperatures look to be in the upper 70s on Tuesday and \nWednesday. The second half of next week looks more unsettled, with a \ncouple frontal passages possible between Wednesday and Friday, as a \ncouple weak waves of low pressure moves across eastern Canada. High \npressure looks to return for the weekend, with drier conditions \nlikely then.\n\nHigh pressure will remain over the forecast area through Tuesday \nnight before shifting offshore by Wednesday. Dry conditions will \npersist with highs on Tuesday into the lower 70s before warming into \nthe middle to upper 70s on Wednesday. Some increase in cloud cover \nwill keep lows warmer with readings into the 40s and 50s. \n\nShower chances then increase Thursday into Thursday night as a \nfrontal boundary and associated weak wave of low pressure drops \nsouth out of Canada. Drier conditions are then possible as we \napproach next weekend.",
